# The document revalidates. Nothing else here needs a rule.
#
# Under Next this was `next.config.ts` `headers()`. Static routes were going out as
# `s-maxage=31536000, stale-while-revalidate`, which tells every shared cache —
# Cloudflare's edge, and whatever proxy sits in front of a link-preview crawler —
# that this HTML is good for a year. It is a marketing page whose whole job is to
# describe a product that changes, and it carries the og:title, og:description and
# og:image every scraper reads. A year-old copy of that is a year-old preview.
#
# Five minutes at the edge, then revalidate, with a day of stale-while-revalidate so
# a reader never waits on the origin. `max-age=0` keeps browsers honest without
# touching the shared cache.
#
# `/` and not `/*`, which is what the Next rule was and what this file said first.
# A `/*` rule matches `/_astro/*` too, and `@astrojs/cloudflare` skips appending its
# own immutable rule for the hashed assets when it finds a matching Cache-Control
# already here — so the catch-all silently downgraded every content-hashed file from
# a year to five minutes. Naming the one document leaves the adapter's rule to be
# generated, which is why there is no `/_astro/*` block below: look in
# `dist/client/_headers` after a build to see it.
#
# The remaining public files — the installer, the OG card, the icon — get no rule and
# so get Cloudflare's asset default, which revalidates against an ETag. That is the
# right answer for `/install` in particular: it is a script people pipe into a shell,
# and it must not be held at an edge for five minutes after a release.

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, s-maxage=300, stale-while-revalidate=86400
